INT. ROLLERAMA SKATE CENTER - MAIN FLOOR - NIGHT

1986. After closing. The disco ball turns slowly, scattering coins of light on an empty maple floor. Speakers hiss. Carpeted benches. Hundreds of rental skates racked on the wall like a choir.

DANI MORALES, 16, Walkman, jean jacket with a band patch, sits on the rail lacing a pair of beat-up quads. She breathes carefully, like someone who has rehearsed this.

INTERCOM (O.S.)
(crackling)
Dani? Is that you out there?

DANI
(startled)
It's me, Mr. Kallis.

INTERCOM (O.S.)
I told you the rink closes at eleven.

DANI
I know. I'm just... finishing.

INTERCOM (O.S.)
Finishing what?

DANI
My last lap.

A long pause on the intercom. A click. A heavy door opens somewhere. Footsteps cross the carpet.

MR. KALLIS, 60s, the owner, cardigan, keys on a retractor, thick glasses, stands at the edge of the floor. He holds a mop like a staff and a Styrofoam cup of cold coffee.

KALLIS
Your last lap.

DANI
Yes, sir.

KALLIS
Your mother called.

DANI
Of course she did.

KALLIS
She thought you might be here.

DANI
I'm always here.

KALLIS
Yes. That's what worries her.

Dani stands on her skates, wobbles, finds the balance. She pushes off the rail, glides ten feet, and stops.

DANI
Mr. Kallis, can I ask you something?

KALLIS
It's your last lap. Ask.

DANI
Do you think it matters? That I'm leaving?

KALLIS
Leaving the rink?

DANI
Leaving town.

Kallis leans the mop on the rail. Takes off his glasses. Cleans them on his cardigan.

KALLIS
Dani, I opened this rink in 1971. Forty kids skated here that first night. Do you know how many of them still live in this town?

DANI
How many?

KALLIS
Two. One of them is the mailman.

DANI
That's sad.

KALLIS
No. It's what a rink is. A place you leave from.

Dani considers that. She rolls closer to him, slow and uncertain.

DANI
My dad skated here.

KALLIS
He did. He was terrible.

DANI
He told me he was great.

KALLIS
He told everyone he was great. That was the whole trouble.

Dani laughs, a short sharp surprise. Then her face crumples. She wipes her nose on her sleeve.

DANI
I don't want to go.

KALLIS
Yes you do.

DANI
How do you know?

KALLIS
Because you came to say goodbye to the floor, not to me.

Dani glances at the floor. At the lights spinning across the maple.

DANI
It's a really good floor.

KALLIS
I refinish it every June.

DANI
I know. I helped you.

KALLIS
So you did.

He crosses to the sound booth, flips a switch. A slow song starts. Something with synthesizers and a long, aching bridge. Dani looks up.

DANI
Mr. Kallis. Is this the couples skate?

KALLIS
It's your last lap. I don't make the rules.

Dani lets out a small, wet laugh and starts to skate. Slowly at first, then with a long glide that carries her all the way around the rink. Kallis, by the booth, turns off the overhead lights. Only the disco ball remains.

DANI
(calling, passing)
Mr. Kallis, am I good?

KALLIS
You are terrible.

DANI
(still skating, grinning)
Just like my dad.

KALLIS
Better.

She comes around again. This time he steps onto the floor in his loafers, arms out, as if to catch her. She skates past, close enough to touch. Their fingertips brush.

The song swells. The ball spins. The old man stays at center floor as the girl circles him, once, twice, a third time, and then drifts toward the exit, where the door is propped open on the cold bright night.

DANI (CONT'D)
Bye, Mr. Kallis.

KALLIS
Bye, Dani.

She doesn't look back. The song fades. The disco ball keeps turning.
